# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# ═══ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ═══
#
# Until v1.4.0 nothing in this repository set `X-Frame-Options` or a
# `frame-ancestors` directive, which means the ENTIRE authenticated application
# could be framed by any site on the internet. That is an ordinary clickjacking
# exposure and it predates embedded signing by four versions. Embedded signing is
# simply the moment it had to be resolved, because the fix ("deny framing") and
# the feature ("allow framing here") are the same header.
#
# Rules are matched most-specific-first by Cloudflare Pages, and a matching rule
# REPLACES the header it names rather than appending to it — so the `/embed/*`
# block below is a complete restatement of the policy for that path, not a
# modifier on the one above it.
#
# ═══ THE HONEST LIMITATION, STATED HERE RATHER THAN IMPLIED AWAY ═══
#
# `frame-ancestors` on `/embed/*` CANNOT be per-tenant. This is a static file
# served by a CDN; it cannot know which API key minted the token in the URL, so
# it cannot narrow the allowed ancestors to the origins that key registered.
# `*` is therefore the honest value, and the defence on that path is NOT the CSP:
#
#   * the credential lives fifteen minutes by default and one hour at most
#   * it is capped at three uses
#   * it was NEVER EMAILED, so it has no mailbox to leak from
#   * it names ONE origin, chosen at mint time from the key owner's allowlist,
#     and the signing page will `postMessage` to that origin and no other. A page
#     framing the ceremony from an unregistered origin receives silence — not a
#     wildcard broadcast (see `utils_Embed_PostMessage.ts`)
#
# What an attacker gains by framing `/embed/sign/{token}` is therefore what they
# already had by opening that URL: they must possess the token. Framing adds no
# capability. Everything OUTSIDE `/embed/*` becomes strictly safer than it was
# before this file existed.
#
# If per-tenant ancestors are ever wanted, the place for them is a dynamic
# response — the Cloudflare Worker that already fronts this product — and not a
# looser value here.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  # Not framing-related, and cheap to state once we are setting headers at all.
  # `nosniff` stops a browser second-guessing a Content-Type, which is how a
  # served file becomes executable script. `no-referrer` matters more than usual
  # on this product: signing URLs carry a bearer credential IN THE PATH, so a
  # referrer header leaving this origin would hand that credential to every
  # third party a signing page ever links or loads from.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

# A signing URL carries a BEARER CREDENTIAL IN ITS PATH. An indexed one is a
# live credential sitting in a search result, which is the same exposure the
# global `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` above exists to prevent on the way out.
# `robots.txt` asks crawlers not to come here; this is the half that does not
# depend on them being polite.
#
# All four security headers are restated, because this file's convention (see the
# top) is that a more-specific block is a COMPLETE restatement of the policy for
# its path rather than a modifier on the block above it.
/sign/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

/embed/*
  # DELIBERATELY PERMISSIVE, and deliberately only here. See the limitation above.
  # `X-Frame-Options` has no wildcard-origin form at all — its only useful values
  # are DENY and SAMEORIGIN, both of which would block the feature — so it is
  # omitted for this path and `frame-ancestors` is the whole policy. Modern
  # browsers prefer `frame-ancestors` over `X-Frame-Options` when both are
  # present; omitting it avoids relying on that preference.
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors *
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  # Same reasoning as /sign/* — an embedded signing token is a credential too.
  # This block keeps its own permissive frame policy; only the crawler
  # directive is added.
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow, noarchive

# The Unicode font the client-side trial fetches at burn time (see
# `utils_PdfBurn_Client.ts`). Unlike bundled assets it is NOT content-hashed —
# it sits at a fixed path so the burn can name it — so caching is opt-in rather
# than automatic. It is 712KB and its bytes never change, which makes a year of
# immutable caching the right answer: a returning visitor pays for it once. If
# the font is ever updated, change the FILE NAME rather than the file, because
# nothing will re-validate this URL for a year.
#
# The four security headers are restated because this file's convention (see the
# top) is that a more-specific block is a COMPLETE restatement of the policy for
# its path, not a modifier on the block above it. `nosniff` is the one that
# actually matters on a font.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
